WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.439 --> 00:00:07.430
Methods are functions that belong to classes,
which are frameworks for objects.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:11.960
Though classes are not part of this exam,
for us to understand the role of a class and

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:16.090
the importance of testing methods for those
classes, let’s look at a brief code example

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.400
defining a class and then an object within
that class.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:28.119
In this 533-methods file, I have a class called
Game_Player, which starts on line 1.

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:33.000
Again, classes are not on the exam, but to
understand method testing, we at least need

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.980
to know what a class is and what it does.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:45.980
This class defines an initial object, starting
on line 2, which contains a character and

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:52.079
a score for a game, and then on lines 3 and
4, we see two properties for the character,

00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:54.000
name, and score.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:02.410
The get_score function on line 5 uses the
character we defined on lines 2 through 4

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:09.479
and then has an interval, set to 10, that
when the code on line 6 runs, the player’s

00:01:09.479 --> 00:01:16.950
score increases 10 points, and then the score
is returned so that it can be used in code

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.579
later if needed.

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:27.049
On line 9, we initiate an instance of a game
player and name the player Robin.

00:01:27.049 --> 00:01:33.150
Then, on lines 10 and 11, we run the get score
method on Robin to add the default value,

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:39.230
10, to Robin’s score and then again to add
100 points to the score.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:43.369
Again, for a test to be successful, we need
to know the expected outcome before we run

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:45.140
said test.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:51.170
I have the expected results in commented code
on lines 10 and 11.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.939
First, we should see 10 as a score.

00:01:53.939 --> 00:02:02.240
Then, on line 11, we override the default
interval of 10 and add 100 to Robin’s score,

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.700
so the total at that point should be 110.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:12.690
I will run this code, and we will get the
expected results of 10 and 110.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:17.010
You may not work with classes right away in
Python, but as you continue to learn this

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:22.130
language, you eventually will, and for that
reason, you need to know how to test methods,

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:26.040
which are functions inside of classes, to
ensure they work as a single class could be

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:31.349
used once, several times, or thousands of
times in code and we want these classes to

00:02:31.349 --> 00:02:35.500
do what their intentions are each time we
call upon them in code.

